WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.327
Orchard Core generates the ID of a content item, and it can differ between sites.

00:00:06.327 --> 00:00:09.878
The Alias part gives an item a name that you choose.

00:00:09.878 --> 00:00:14.226
In this video, we add the part to a content type, create aliases,

00:00:14.226 --> 00:00:16.430
and see the rules they follow.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.427
A content item ID is a string that Orchard Core generates.

00:00:24.427 --> 00:00:28.248
The same menu can have a different ID on your development site and

00:00:28.248 --> 00:00:32.600
on your production site, so nothing that moves between sites can rely on it.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.817
An alias is a name that you choose, such as main-menu.

00:00:36.817 --> 00:00:42.190
It is stored with the content item, so recipes and deployments carry it to other sites.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.390
An alias is not a web address, because a page address comes from the Autoroute part.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.306
Let's open Tools, then Features, and search for alias.

00:00:55.306 --> 00:01:00.589
The Alias feature is listed under Content Management and is enabled, because the

00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:02.432
Blog recipe turns it on.

00:01:02.432 --> 00:01:04.890
On another site, you click Enable.

00:01:09.095 --> 00:01:14.502
Let's open Design, Content Definition, Content Types, and click Edit on the Article type.

00:01:14.502 --> 00:01:18.201
A type is made of parts, and its Parts list shows the parts

00:01:18.201 --> 00:01:19.965
that Article already has.

00:01:20.416 --> 00:01:21.706
We click Add Parts.

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:25.111
The page lists the parts that can be added, and we click Add

00:01:25.111 --> 00:01:26.916
& Configure on the Alias row.

00:01:27.371 --> 00:01:30.767
The settings of the part open on their own page.

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:33.472
Options decides who writes the alias:

00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:37.937
with Alias is editable, an editor can type one, and with Alias is

00:01:37.937 --> 00:01:42.151
generated and input is disabled, the editor cannot change it.

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:47.211
Pattern is the template that builds an alias while the alias is empty.

00:01:47.211 --> 00:01:52.913
The default uses the display text of the item, in lowercase, with hyphens between words.

00:01:52.913 --> 00:01:55.461
We keep the defaults and click Save.

00:01:59.666 --> 00:02:05.934
Under Content, Content Items, we choose New, then Article, and type the title Contact Us.

00:02:05.934 --> 00:02:09.035
The Alias field is further down the page.

00:02:09.035 --> 00:02:13.126
We leave it empty, because Orchard Core can generate it.

00:02:13.585 --> 00:02:16.413
We click Publish, and then edit the item again.

00:02:16.413 --> 00:02:20.894
The Alias field holds contact-us, which Orchard Core built from the title when

00:02:20.894 --> 00:02:22.175
it saved the item.

00:02:22.628 --> 00:02:25.905
Let's change the title to Contact and publish again.

00:02:25.905 --> 00:02:31.611
The alias stays contact-us, because Orchard Core builds an alias only while the field is empty.

00:02:31.611 --> 00:02:33.928
That is what keeps an alias stable.

00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:38.272
To generate an alias again, we clear the field and publish.

00:02:38.272 --> 00:02:42.764
Orchard Core builds the alias from the title Contact, so when we open

00:02:42.764 --> 00:02:45.519
the item again, the field holds contact.

00:02:49.723 --> 00:02:52.040
An editor can also type an alias.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.962
We create an Article titled Help Center and enter main-menu.

00:02:55.962 --> 00:03:00.656
When we publish, the editor shows an error and saves nothing, because the

00:03:00.656 --> 00:03:02.973
Main Menu already has that alias.

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:13.301
To stop editors from changing aliases, we edit the Alias part of the Article type again.

00:03:13.301 --> 00:03:18.829
We choose Alias is generated and input is disabled for Options, and click Save.

00:03:19.289 --> 00:03:21.357
Let's open the About article.

00:03:21.357 --> 00:03:26.263
Its Alias field is disabled and empty, because About existed before the part.

00:03:26.263 --> 00:03:31.169
We click Publish and open the article again, and the Alias field holds about.

00:03:35.369 --> 00:03:40.859
An alias belongs to one content item on the whole site, whatever its content type.

00:03:40.859 --> 00:03:45.288
A generated alias that is already taken gets a number at the end.

00:03:45.288 --> 00:03:49.592
An alias holds up to 735 characters, and finding an item by its

00:03:49.592 --> 00:03:51.589
alias ignores letter case.

00:03:55.781 --> 00:04:00.093
The Menu feature depends on Alias, so menus have an alias too.

00:04:00.093 --> 00:04:05.609
Under Content, Menus, we edit the Main Menu, and its Alias field holds main-menu.

00:04:05.609 --> 00:04:07.321
We leave it as it is.

00:04:07.776 --> 00:04:11.229
The layout of the Blog theme asks for the menu by this alias.

00:04:11.229 --> 00:04:14.836
On the front end, the Home and About links in the navigation bar

00:04:14.836 --> 00:04:16.176
come from that menu.

00:04:18.176 --> 00:04:22.026
The Alias part gives a content item a name that stays with it

00:04:22.026 --> 00:04:23.405
from site to site.

00:04:23.405 --> 00:04:28.462
Menus, templates, and queries can find the item by that name, instead of by an ID.

00:04:28.462 --> 00:04:29.956
Thanks for watching.

